export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"modelcraft","addon":"flow","name":"Material flow","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"flow-start","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: getting started","summary":"What the Material flow add-on does in ModelCraft, how to switch it on, and a first run with the example line.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["material flow","material handling","conveyor","simulation","throughput","warehouse","distribution center","agv","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"a-first-run","text":"A first run","level":2},{"id":"where-the-layout-is-kept","text":"Where the layout is kept","level":2},{"id":"what-it-is-not","text":"What it is not","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMaterial flow is an add-on for ModelCraft that comes with it and is free. You lay out a material-handling system in the 3D view — conveyors, curves, inclines, zone conveyors, merges, diverts, stop gates, photo-eyes, stations, buffers and AGV paths — and run a simulation of it: loads arrive, travel, queue, get worked on and leave, and the add-on tells you the throughput, how long loads take, how busy each station is and where queues build up. You can play the run back in the 3D view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you switch it on: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial flow\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"a-first-run\"\u003EA first run\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExample line\u003C/span\u003E. A source, a conveyor, a divert, two lanes with a station each, a merge and a sink appear in the 3D view, and the view zooms to them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. Eight hours are simulated; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResults\u003C/span\u003E shows loads finished, loads per hour and lead times.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlay\u003C/span\u003E and choose a speed (60× shows a minute every second). Loads move along the belts, wait in front of the stations and take turns at the merge.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen a station under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResults\u003C/span\u003E to see its utilization and queue. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave results as CSV\u003C/span\u003E for a spreadsheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChange something — a station&#039;s time, a belt&#039;s speed, the divert&#039;s rule — and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E again to compare.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"where-the-layout-is-kept\"\u003EWhere the layout is kept\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is kept in this browser. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave…\u003C/span\u003E writes it to a \u003Ccode\u003E.ccflow.json\u003C/code\u003E file and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen…\u003C/span\u003E reads one back; share that file to share a layout. It is not saved inside the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is-not\"\u003EWhat it is not\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELoads are moved by rules, not by physics: they never tip, slide or fall, and they keep their place on a belt. The simulation is a planning tool: it shows what a layout can carry and where it backs up. Connecting a real PLC to the model is planned for CadCraft Studio later; the web edition simulates only.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Material flow is an add-on for ModelCraft that comes with it and is free. You lay out a material-handling system in the 3D view — conveyors, curves, inclines, zone conveyors, merges, diverts, stop gates, photo-eyes, stations, buffers and AGV paths — and run a simulation of it: loads arrive, travel, queue, get worked on and leave, and the add-on tells you the throughput, how long loads take, how busy each station is and where queues build up. You can play the run back in the 3D view. It is off until you switch it on: Settings › General › Add-ons › Material flow. Its tools are in the Material flow section of the right panel. A first run Press Example line. A source, a conveyor, a divert, two lanes with a station each, a merge and a sink appear in the 3D view, and the view zooms to them. Press Run. Eight hours are simulated; Results shows loads finished, loads per hour and lead times. Press Play and choose a speed (60× shows a minute every second). Loads move along the belts, wait in front of the stations and take turns at the merge. Open a station under Results to see its utilization and queue. Press Save results as CSV for a spreadsheet. Change something — a station's time, a belt's speed, the divert's rule — and press Run again to compare. Where the layout is kept The layout is kept in this browser. Save… writes it to a .ccflow.json file and Open… reads one back; share that file to share a layout. It is not saved inside the .modelcraft file yet. What it is not Loads are moved by rules, not by physics: they never tip, slide or fall, and they keep their place on a belt. The simulation is a planning tool: it shows what a layout can carry and where it backs up. Connecting a real PLC to the model is planned for CadCraft Studio later; the web edition simulates only."},{"slug":"flow-layout","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: lay out a system","summary":"Add items from the catalog, place and move them with the mouse, join them port to port, and set their parameters.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["layout","catalog","port","snap","conveyor","curve","incline","merge","divert","gate","photo-eye","station","buffer","source","sink","agv path"],"headings":[{"id":"ports","text":"Ports","level":2},{"id":"add-items","text":"Add items","level":2},{"id":"place-and-move-with-the-mouse","text":"Place and move with the mouse","level":2},{"id":"the-catalog","text":"The catalog","level":2},{"id":"times","text":"Times","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"ports\"\u003EPorts\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery item has ports: an input where loads come in and one or more outputs where they leave, each with a direction. Two items are joined when an output and an input touch and point the same way. Joints are never stored: they are worked out from where things are, so moving an item and snapping it back keeps the flow right. Free ports show as small red markers in the 3D view.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"add-items\"\u003EAdd items\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd from the catalog\u003C/span\u003E, choose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EItem\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EJoin to\u003C/span\u003E, choose the free output it should follow (the selected item&#039;s output is chosen for you). The new item is placed with its input on that output, facing the same way.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd\u003C/span\u003E. It is selected, and its parameters show below.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"place-and-move-with-the-mouse\"\u003EPlace and move with the mouse\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlace with mouse\u003C/span\u003E and click in the 3D view: the chosen item lands on the ground point you clicked and, when a free port is within 0.6 m, joins it. Keep clicking to place more; press Esc (or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop placing\u003C/span\u003E) to stop.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EDrag any item to move it: it slides over the ground at its own height and snaps onto a free port within 0.6 m when you let go. \u003Cstrong\u003EHold Shift\u003C/strong\u003E to drag it together with everything joined to it (belts, ports, AGV paths and the photo-eyes on them), so a whole line moves as one. Untick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMove and place with the mouse\u003C/span\u003E to leave the 3D view to ModelCraft&#039;s own tools. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EUndo\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERedo\u003C/span\u003E step back through the changes you made here (the last 60).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe layout is saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file with the model, and opens with it. Where the add-on is off, or not installed, the file still keeps the layout untouched.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENothing (place it free)\u003C/span\u003E to place an item on its own; then set its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EX\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EHeight\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn\u003C/span\u003E and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESnap\u003C/span\u003E: it jumps onto the nearest free port within 0.6 m.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-catalog\"\u003EThe catalog\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EItem\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConveyor, curve, incline\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA belt. Loads move at its speed and keep the gap you set. With \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAccumulate\u003C/span\u003E on, loads queue up nose to tail when the end is blocked; off, the whole belt stops. A curve turns left for a positive angle.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EZone conveyor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAn accumulating belt in zones of equal length: one load a zone.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMerge\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETwo or three inputs, one output. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPriority\u003C/span\u003E lets input 1 go first; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFirst come\u003C/span\u003E the load that has waited longest; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETake turns\u003C/span\u003E alternates.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDivert\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOne input, two or three outputs (straight on, left, right). \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERound-robin\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFirst free\u003C/span\u003E (an output that can take the load now, in order), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy load type\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EA 1, B 2\u003C/code\u003E), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy share\u003C/span\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E1 3\u003C/code\u003E sends a quarter to output 1) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBy photo-eye\u003C/span\u003E (a lane whose photo-eye has a load standing on it counts as full).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETransfer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHands a load across in a set time, one at a time.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStop gate\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHolds loads: open, closed, or open and closed in turn.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStation\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWorks on loads: servers, a queue in front, and the time each load takes. A load that is done but cannot leave keeps its server (blocked).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBuffer\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHolds a number of loads, first in, first out.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESource\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMakes loads: a time between loads, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAlways one waiting\u003C/span\u003E to find the most a layout can carry. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELoad types\u003C/span\u003E is a list of type, share and length (\u003Ccode\u003EA 3 0.4, B 1 0.6\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESink\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhere loads leave and are counted.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPhoto-eye\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESits on a conveyor at a distance along it. It counts loads and tells a divert when a load stands on it.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EAGV path, pickup, drop\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESee &quot;Material flow: AGVs&quot;.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"times\"\u003ETimes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA time can be fixed or follow a distribution: uniform (least and most), exponential (mean: random arrivals), normal (mean and standard deviation, never below zero), triangular (least, most likely, most) or Erlang (mean and phases). All times are in seconds, lengths in meters.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Ports Every item has ports: an input where loads come in and one or more outputs where they leave, each with a direction. Two items are joined when an output and an input touch and point the same way. Joints are never stored: they are worked out from where things are, so moving an item and snapping it back keeps the flow right. Free ports show as small red markers in the 3D view. Add items Under Add from the catalog, choose the Item. In Join to, choose the free output it should follow (the selected item's output is chosen for you). The new item is placed with its input on that output, facing the same way. Press Add. It is selected, and its parameters show below. Place and move with the mouse Press Place with mouse and click in the 3D view: the chosen item lands on the ground point you clicked and, when a free port is within 0.6 m, joins it. Keep clicking to place more; press Esc (or Stop placing) to stop. Drag any item to move it: it slides over the ground at its own height and snaps onto a free port within 0.6 m when you let go. Hold Shift to drag it together with everything joined to it (belts, ports, AGV paths and the photo-eyes on them), so a whole line moves as one. Untick Move and place with the mouse to leave the 3D view to ModelCraft's own tools. Undo and Redo step back through the changes you made here (the last 60). The layout is saved in the .modelcraft file with the model, and opens with it. Where the add-on is off, or not installed, the file still keeps the layout untouched. Choose Nothing (place it free) to place an item on its own; then set its X, Y, Height and Turn and press Snap: it jumps onto the nearest free port within 0.6 m. The catalog Item What it does Conveyor, curve, incline A belt. Loads move at its speed and keep the gap you set. With Accumulate on, loads queue up nose to tail when the end is blocked; off, the whole belt stops. A curve turns left for a positive angle. Zone conveyor An accumulating belt in zones of equal length: one load a zone. Merge Two or three inputs, one output. Priority lets input 1 go first; First come the load that has waited longest; Take turns alternates. Divert One input, two or three outputs (straight on, left, right). Round-robin, First free (an output that can take the load now, in order), By load type (A 1, B 2), By share (1 3 sends a quarter to output 1) or By photo-eye (a lane whose photo-eye has a load standing on it counts as full). Transfer Hands a load across in a set time, one at a time. Stop gate Holds loads: open, closed, or open and closed in turn. Station Works on loads: servers, a queue in front, and the time each load takes. A load that is done but cannot leave keeps its server (blocked). Buffer Holds a number of loads, first in, first out. Source Makes loads: a time between loads, or Always one waiting to find the most a layout can carry. Load types is a list of type, share and length (A 3 0.4, B 1 0.6). Sink Where loads leave and are counted. Photo-eye Sits on a conveyor at a distance along it. It counts loads and tells a divert when a load stands on it. AGV path, pickup, drop See \"Material flow: AGVs\". Times A time can be fixed or follow a distribution: uniform (least and most), exponential (mean: random arrivals), normal (mean and standard deviation, never below zero), triangular (least, most likely, most) or Erlang (mean and phases). All times are in seconds, lengths in meters."},{"slug":"flow-agv","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: AGVs","summary":"Lay out one-way or two-way AGV paths, a pickup and a drop, set the fleet, and read what the vehicles did.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["agv","automated guided vehicle","amr","path","pickup","drop","fleet","reservation","deadlock","zone control"],"headings":[{"id":"lay-out-the-paths","text":"Lay out the paths","level":2},{"id":"the-fleet","text":"The fleet","level":2},{"id":"how-the-vehicles-behave","text":"How the vehicles behave","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"lay-out-the-paths\"\u003ELay out the paths\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAGV paths are one-way segments, or two-way lanes when you tick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETwo-way lane\u003C/span\u003E. Add an \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAGV path\u003C/span\u003E joined to a pickup or to the end of another path; set its \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELength\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETurn\u003C/span\u003E so it goes where you want. Segments whose ends touch (within 5 cm) form one network. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExample AGVs\u003C/span\u003E for a square loop with a pickup, a drop and two vehicles.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAGV pickup\u003C/span\u003E takes loads from a conveyor; its marker (beyond its front) must sit on a path end. An \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAGV drop\u003C/span\u003E puts loads onto a conveyor; its marker (behind it) must also sit on a path end. Set the pickup&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDeliver to\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-fleet\"\u003EThe fleet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAGV fleet\u003C/span\u003E sets how many vehicles there are, their speed, and how long loading and unloading take. Vehicles start at the ends of segments spread around the network.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"how-the-vehicles-behave\"\u003EHow the vehicles behave\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA load waiting at a pickup calls the nearest free vehicle (by distance along the paths).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA vehicle drives on a one-way segment only when no other vehicle is on it: it waits at the end of its segment until the next one is clear. Two vehicles are never on one segment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA free vehicle in another&#039;s way moves on to the next segment.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Etwo-way lane\u003C/strong\u003E is one reservation for both directions: only one vehicle at a time drives on it, either way. A vehicle is on the lane only while it drives along it; once it has arrived it stands at the node and the lane is free again (vehicles may pass each other at a node). A vehicle that turns back onto a lane another vehicle is waiting for hands it over first, so none is starved.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWith \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EReserve junctions\u003C/span\u003E on, where three or more lanes meet only one vehicle at a time may be on the approach to the junction or standing at it, so paths never cross with two vehicles at the junction.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIf vehicles end up waiting for each other in a circle, the run stops and says which vehicles and where. A free vehicle at the end of a lane with nowhere to move aside stops the run too (stuck). Add segments (a loop or a passing place) or use fewer vehicles.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResults\u003C/span\u003E shows, for each vehicle, the loads delivered and how its time was spent: driving loaded, driving empty, waiting and idle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ECharging, vehicle sizes and traffic rules beyond lanes and junctions are not built yet.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Lay out the paths AGV paths are one-way segments, or two-way lanes when you tick Two-way lane. Add an AGV path joined to a pickup or to the end of another path; set its Length and Turn so it goes where you want. Segments whose ends touch (within 5 cm) form one network. Press Example AGVs for a square loop with a pickup, a drop and two vehicles. An AGV pickup takes loads from a conveyor; its marker (beyond its front) must sit on a path end. An AGV drop puts loads onto a conveyor; its marker (behind it) must also sit on a path end. Set the pickup's Deliver to. The fleet AGV fleet sets how many vehicles there are, their speed, and how long loading and unloading take. Vehicles start at the ends of segments spread around the network. How the vehicles behave A load waiting at a pickup calls the nearest free vehicle (by distance along the paths). A vehicle drives on a one-way segment only when no other vehicle is on it: it waits at the end of its segment until the next one is clear. Two vehicles are never on one segment. A free vehicle in another's way moves on to the next segment. A two-way lane is one reservation for both directions: only one vehicle at a time drives on it, either way. A vehicle is on the lane only while it drives along it; once it has arrived it stands at the node and the lane is free again (vehicles may pass each other at a node). A vehicle that turns back onto a lane another vehicle is waiting for hands it over first, so none is starved. With Reserve junctions on, where three or more lanes meet only one vehicle at a time may be on the approach to the junction or standing at it, so paths never cross with two vehicles at the junction. If vehicles end up waiting for each other in a circle, the run stops and says which vehicles and where. A free vehicle at the end of a lane with nowhere to move aside stops the run too (stuck). Add segments (a loop or a passing place) or use fewer vehicles. Results shows, for each vehicle, the loads delivered and how its time was spent: driving loaded, driving empty, waiting and idle. Charging, vehicle sizes and traffic rules beyond lanes and junctions are not built yet."},{"slug":"flow-shifts","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: shifts, breakdowns and live runs","summary":"Give stations and sources a weekly shift calendar, let stations and belts break down, and watch a run at the pace of the clock.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["shift","calendar","breakdown","mtbf","mttr","availability","live","real time","worker","cancel","progress"],"headings":[{"id":"shift-calendars","text":"Shift calendars","level":2},{"id":"breakdowns","text":"Breakdowns","level":2},{"id":"operator-pools-and-parking","text":"Operator pools and parking","level":2},{"id":"run-cancel-and-live","text":"Run, Cancel and Live","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"shift-calendars\"\u003EShift calendars\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShift calendars\u003C/span\u003E press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd a shift calendar\u003C/span\u003E. A calendar is one week that repeats: windows of &quot;on&quot; time, each on some days from a start to an end time. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFill from\u003C/span\u003E offers common patterns (one, two or three shifts, every day). An end time earlier than the start runs past midnight (22:00 to 06:00 on Monday ends Tuesday 06:00). Time 0 of a run is \u003Cstrong\u003EMonday 00:00\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose the calendar in a station&#039;s or a source&#039;s \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShift calendar\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Estation\u003C/strong\u003E starts no new work while its calendar is off. Work in progress finishes. Loads wait in its queue (up to the queue size) and the line backs up behind it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA \u003Cstrong\u003Esource\u003C/strong\u003E makes nothing while its calendar is off; the first load comes the moment it turns on again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EResults for such a station show how much of the run it was open.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"breakdowns\"\u003EBreakdowns\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOn a station, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ETime between breakdowns (0 = never)\u003C/span\u003E (hours of clock time, drawn from an exponential distribution) and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMean repair time\u003C/span\u003E (minutes, an Erlang-2 distribution) make it break down now and then. While it is broken nothing starts and work in progress pauses, then continues with the time it had left. Long-run availability is the time between breakdowns divided by that plus the repair time. Results show the share of time available and the number of breakdowns. Everything is seeded: the same seed gives the same breakdowns.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EBelts (straight, curved and inclined conveyors) have the same two settings. A belt that breaks down stops: every load on it stays where it is, nothing gets on or off, and after the repair it runs on from where it stopped (playback shows it standing still). Gates break down too: a broken gate sticks shut, so loads wait in front of it until the repair. Vehicles have \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVehicle time between breakdowns (0 = never)\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EVehicle mean repair time\u003C/span\u003E in the AGV fleet: a vehicle that is due to break down stops where it next stands (the end of the path it is on, or where it waits), keeps the lane it holds, and carries on with its job after the repair; results show each vehicle&#039;s availability and breakdowns. Zone conveyors do not break down yet.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"operator-pools-and-parking\"\u003EOperator pools and parking\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOperator pools\u003C/span\u003E press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd an operator pool\u003C/span\u003E, name it and say how many people it has. Give a station that pool in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOperator pool\u003C/span\u003E: each load it serves needs one person from the pool for the whole service, so with fewer people than servers some servers stand idle, and stations that share a pool take turns when a person comes free. Results show the pool&#039;s use. In the AGV fleet, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EParking places\u003C/span\u003E lists AGV paths where idle vehicles with no job wait, one place each, instead of blocking a lane.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"run-cancel-and-live\"\u003ERun, Cancel and Live\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E simulates in a worker so the page stays usable. A line shows how much of the run is done; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E stops it. Where a browser cannot start a worker, the run happens on the page and the message says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELive\u003C/span\u003E runs the model at the pace of the clock: at 1× one model second takes one second (pick a faster speed to speed it up) and loads and vehicles move in the 3D view as it goes. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPause\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResume\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStop live\u003C/span\u003E are next to it. When the run reaches its end the results and playback are there as after a Run. The result is the same as a Run with the same seed.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Shift calendars Under Shift calendars press Add a shift calendar. A calendar is one week that repeats: windows of \"on\" time, each on some days from a start to an end time. Fill from offers common patterns (one, two or three shifts, every day). An end time earlier than the start runs past midnight (22:00 to 06:00 on Monday ends Tuesday 06:00). Time 0 of a run is Monday 00:00. Choose the calendar in a station's or a source's Shift calendar. A station starts no new work while its calendar is off. Work in progress finishes. Loads wait in its queue (up to the queue size) and the line backs up behind it. A source makes nothing while its calendar is off; the first load comes the moment it turns on again. Results for such a station show how much of the run it was open. Breakdowns On a station, Time between breakdowns (0 = never) (hours of clock time, drawn from an exponential distribution) and Mean repair time (minutes, an Erlang-2 distribution) make it break down now and then. While it is broken nothing starts and work in progress pauses, then continues with the time it had left. Long-run availability is the time between breakdowns divided by that plus the repair time. Results show the share of time available and the number of breakdowns. Everything is seeded: the same seed gives the same breakdowns. Belts (straight, curved and inclined conveyors) have the same two settings. A belt that breaks down stops: every load on it stays where it is, nothing gets on or off, and after the repair it runs on from where it stopped (playback shows it standing still). Gates break down too: a broken gate sticks shut, so loads wait in front of it until the repair. Vehicles have Vehicle time between breakdowns (0 = never) and Vehicle mean repair time in the AGV fleet: a vehicle that is due to break down stops where it next stands (the end of the path it is on, or where it waits), keeps the lane it holds, and carries on with its job after the repair; results show each vehicle's availability and breakdowns. Zone conveyors do not break down yet. Operator pools and parking Under Operator pools press Add an operator pool, name it and say how many people it has. Give a station that pool in Operator pool: each load it serves needs one person from the pool for the whole service, so with fewer people than servers some servers stand idle, and stations that share a pool take turns when a person comes free. Results show the pool's use. In the AGV fleet, Parking places lists AGV paths where idle vehicles with no job wait, one place each, instead of blocking a lane. Run, Cancel and Live Run simulates in a worker so the page stays usable. A line shows how much of the run is done; Cancel stops it. Where a browser cannot start a worker, the run happens on the page and the message says so. Live runs the model at the pace of the clock: at 1× one model second takes one second (pick a faster speed to speed it up) and loads and vehicles move in the 3D view as it goes. Pause, Resume and Stop live are next to it. When the run reaches its end the results and playback are there as after a Run. The result is the same as a Run with the same seed."},{"slug":"flow-results","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: results and how the simulation works","summary":"What each result means, how warm-up and the random seed work, the CSV, and the known answers the simulation is tested against.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["results","statistics","throughput","lead time","utilization","queue","warm-up","seed","csv","discrete event","queueing theory"],"headings":[{"id":"results","text":"Results","level":2},{"id":"warm-up-and-the-seed","text":"Warm-up and the seed","level":2},{"id":"playback","text":"Playback","level":2},{"id":"csv","text":"CSV","level":2},{"id":"tested-against-known-answers","text":"Tested against known answers","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"results\"\u003EResults\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EResult\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoads finished, per hour\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoads that reached a sink after the warm-up.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELead time\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFrom a load being made at its source to leaving at a sink: mean, 95th percentile (95 in 100 took less) and the longest.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn the system at the end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoads still on belts, in queues or on vehicles when the run ends.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EUtilization\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EThe share of time a station&#039;s servers were working. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EBlocked\u003C/span\u003E: done, but the way on was full.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EQueue, wait in queue\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoads waiting in front of a station, averaged over time, and how long each waited.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELoads on it, end blocked\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFor a belt: how many loads were on it, and the share of time its end was held.\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"warm-up-and-the-seed\"\u003EWarm-up and the seed\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA run starts empty. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWarm-up\u003C/span\u003E minutes are simulated but not counted, so the results describe the system once it is full. Random times use the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERandom seed\u003C/span\u003E: the same layout and seed give exactly the same results on every computer; another seed gives another, equally likely day. Run a few seeds before trusting a small difference.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEach item draws its own random numbers, so adding an item elsewhere does not change what the others draw.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"playback\"\u003EPlayback\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe first two hours of a run are kept for playback; the results cover the whole run. On a belt a load is drawn where the simulation put it; in a queue, in a row before the station.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"csv\"\u003ECSV\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave results as CSV\u003C/span\u003E writes one row per element and measure, with full precision: the run (seed, hours, totals, lead times), each load type, each element and each vehicle.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tested-against-known-answers\"\u003ETested against known answers\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe simulation is checked against queueing theory and simple cases worked out by hand, among them: a station with random arrivals and a fixed time at 80 % load (mean wait 16 s for an 8 s job, within 4 %), the same with random times on one and two servers, a belt&#039;s travel time and capacity (a 10 m belt with 0.4 m loads 0.1 m apart holds 20), loads leaving a released queue one spacing apart, merge priority, and every divert rule. Shifts and breakdowns are checked the same way: a station open one hour of two completes exactly the loads that fit in that hour, and a saturated station that breaks down follows availability = time between breakdowns ÷ (that + repair time) within 3 %.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Results Result Means Loads finished, per hour Loads that reached a sink after the warm-up. Lead time From a load being made at its source to leaving at a sink: mean, 95th percentile (95 in 100 took less) and the longest. In the system at the end Loads still on belts, in queues or on vehicles when the run ends. Utilization The share of time a station's servers were working. Blocked: done, but the way on was full. Queue, wait in queue Loads waiting in front of a station, averaged over time, and how long each waited. Loads on it, end blocked For a belt: how many loads were on it, and the share of time its end was held. Warm-up and the seed A run starts empty. Warm-up minutes are simulated but not counted, so the results describe the system once it is full. Random times use the Random seed: the same layout and seed give exactly the same results on every computer; another seed gives another, equally likely day. Run a few seeds before trusting a small difference. Each item draws its own random numbers, so adding an item elsewhere does not change what the others draw. Playback The first two hours of a run are kept for playback; the results cover the whole run. On a belt a load is drawn where the simulation put it; in a queue, in a row before the station. CSV Save results as CSV writes one row per element and measure, with full precision: the run (seed, hours, totals, lead times), each load type, each element and each vehicle. Tested against known answers The simulation is checked against queueing theory and simple cases worked out by hand, among them: a station with random arrivals and a fixed time at 80 % load (mean wait 16 s for an 8 s job, within 4 %), the same with random times on one and two servers, a belt's travel time and capacity (a 10 m belt with 0.4 m loads 0.1 m apart holds 20), loads leaving a released queue one spacing apart, merge priority, and every divert rule. Shifts and breakdowns are checked the same way: a station open one hour of two completes exactly the loads that fit in that hour, and a saturated station that breaks down follows availability = time between breakdowns ÷ (that + repair time) within 3 %."},{"slug":"flow-limits","scope":"addon:flow","title":"Material flow: limits and what is not built yet","summary":"What the Material flow add-on does not do yet, and the limits of this version.","section":"trouble","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not built","physics","plc","emulation","modbus","opc ua","robot","crane","worker","live"],"headings":[{"id":"limits-of-this-version","text":"Limits of this version","level":2},{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"limits-of-this-version\"\u003ELimits of this version\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENo physics.\u003C/strong\u003E Loads are moved by rules; they never tip, collide or fall. A belt moves loads at its speed; acceleration is not modeled.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBelts are joined by touching ports.\u003C/strong\u003E The layout is saved in the \u003Ccode\u003E.modelcraft\u003C/code\u003E file; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave…\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EOpen…\u003C/span\u003E also keep it as a separate file.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EAGVs:\u003C/strong\u003E one-way segments and two-way lanes with junction reservation; no battery charging or vehicle sizes yet; parking is one place per vehicle on a path you name. Head-on traffic in a single corridor with no way round stops the run and says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBreakdowns are for stations, belts, gates and vehicles\u003C/strong\u003E (a time between breakdowns and a mean repair time); zone conveyors do not break down yet. A vehicle&#039;s breakdown takes effect where it next stands, not in the middle of a path. Operator pools serve stations only (one person per service; no skills, breaks or walking time).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA run goes to a worker, with a progress line and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECancel\u003C/span\u003E; where a browser cannot start a worker it runs on the page and says so. Very long runs stop at an event limit and say so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELive\u003C/strong\u003E keeps the first 200 000 load positions of a run for the 3D view; after that, loads stop being drawn (the numbers go on).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EConnecting a PLC to the model (emulation): sensors as PLC inputs, motors and diverts as PLC outputs. Planned for CadCraft Studio, which can talk to a PLC; the web edition will keep simulating only.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERobots, cranes, shuttles and lifts; pallet and tote stacks; walking time and skills for operators.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Limits of this version No physics. Loads are moved by rules; they never tip, collide or fall. A belt moves loads at its speed; acceleration is not modeled. Belts are joined by touching ports. The layout is saved in the .modelcraft file; Save… and Open… also keep it as a separate file. AGVs: one-way segments and two-way lanes with junction reservation; no battery charging or vehicle sizes yet; parking is one place per vehicle on a path you name. Head-on traffic in a single corridor with no way round stops the run and says so. Breakdowns are for stations, belts, gates and vehicles (a time between breakdowns and a mean repair time); zone conveyors do not break down yet. A vehicle's breakdown takes effect where it next stands, not in the middle of a path. Operator pools serve stations only (one person per service; no skills, breaks or walking time). A run goes to a worker, with a progress line and Cancel; where a browser cannot start a worker it runs on the page and says so. Very long runs stop at an event limit and say so. Live keeps the first 200 000 load positions of a run for the 3D view; after that, loads stop being drawn (the numbers go on). Not built yet Connecting a PLC to the model (emulation): sensors as PLC inputs, motors and diverts as PLC outputs. Planned for CadCraft Studio, which can talk to a PLC; the web edition will keep simulating only. Robots, cranes, shuttles and lifts; pallet and tote stacks; walking time and skills for operators."}]};
